{"event":"intent","sources":["origin","f001"],"description":"SNAPSHOT=s01; BRANCH=debugfs-write-memdup-sscanf; Direct structural/API analogue of the seed (scsi bfa bfad_debugfs regrd/regwr). Search only snapshots/s01 and no other snapshot. Locate debugfs and analogous debug-control .write file_operations handlers that copy attacker-controlled user data via memdup_user or kmalloc(count)+copy_from_user into an exactly nbytes-sized heap buffer, then pass the possibly-unterminated buffer to C-string consumers (sscanf, simple_strtoul/simple_strtol, kstrtol, strlen, strcmp, strchr, strscpy) with no memdup_user_nul, strndup_user, or count+1 terminator slot. Grep debugfs_create_file and .write call sites, memdup_user uses, and sscanf/simple_strtoul on user-copied buffers. For each candidate record file:line, the copy call with its exact size argument, the string use, and terminator evidence; reject with concrete evidence where termination or bounds are enforced. A negative evidence-backed result is valid. Save a structured JSON artifact for snapshot s01 only."}
{"event":"intent","sources":["origin","f001"],"description":"SNAPSHOT=s01; BRANCH=sysfs-store-attr-terminator; Same usercopy-then-string-parser invariant under a different interface name. Search only snapshots/s01 and no other snapshot. Locate sysfs ->store() callbacks (DEVICE_ATTR and _RW variants, DRIVER_ATTR, BUS_ATTR, CLASS_ATTR, module_param handlers) and kernfs-backed attribute stores that (a) perform their own copy_from_user/memdup_user of attacker-chosen length into a fixed- or exactly-sized buffer and then use sscanf/simple_strtol, strcmp, strlen, strchr, or strscpy on possibly-unterminated data, or (b) parse buf while trusting count over the real string length, or index buf[count]. Verify whether the kernfs page-buffer trailing-NUL guarantee actually holds at each use site and reject candidates where termination and bounds are already guaranteed. Record file:line, copy primitive, size, string use, and terminator evidence; negatives with evidence are valid. Save a structured JSON artifact for snapshot s01 only."}
{"event":"intent","sources":["origin","f001"],"description":"SNAPSHOT=s01; BRANCH=proc-config-write-unterminated; Trust-boundary and state-transition analogue through other textual control-file write paths. Search only snapshots/s01 and no other snapshot. Locate /proc write handlers (proc_ops .proc_write, file_operations .write on proc entries), configfs ->store_* item/attribute callbacks, securityfs/tracefs control files, and misc textual control writers that copy user data of attacker-chosen length (memdup_user, kmalloc(len)+copy_from_user, copy_from_user into stack arrays) and then consume it with string functions assuming NUL termination (sscanf, simple_strtoul, strlen, strcmp, strchr, strscpy, strcpy), especially where the allocation size equals the user length exactly with no +1 terminator slot. Record file:line, copy primitive and size argument, string use, and terminator evidence; reject candidates with proper termination or non-string consumption, with evidence. Save a structured JSON artifact for snapshot s01 only."}
{"event":"intent","sources":["origin","f001"],"description":"SNAPSHOT=s01; BRANCH=ioctl-sock-usercopy-string; Cross-subsystem, alternative-syntax analogue of the seed invariant. Search only snapshots/s01 and no other snapshot. Locate ioctl handlers (.unlocked_ioctl, .compat_ioctl), setsockopt/getsockopt optval parsers, netlink attribute handling (nla_data/nla_string consumed without NLA_NUL_STRING policy guarantees), and char-device .write fops that copy_from_user into variable- or exactly-sized buffers and then run string helpers (sscanf, simple_strtoul/simple_strtol, strcmp, strchr, strscpy, strlen) on the unterminated result. Grep copy_from_user and memdup_user call sites near ioctl/sockopt implementations followed by string ops. Record file:line, copy call, size versus user length, string use, and terminator evidence; reject candidates where a terminator or bounds check exists. Save a structured JSON artifact for snapshot s01 only."}
